const lessons = [
 {id:"framework",title:"A repeatable system design framework",track:"System design",type:"Guide",time:"20 min",level:"START HERE",desc:"A clear sequence for requirements, estimation, APIs, data models, architecture, and bottlenecks.",sections:["Clarify the functional requirements and non-functional targets: scale, latency, availability, durability, and consistency. State what is out of scope.","Estimate orders of magnitude: active users, requests per second, read/write ratio, payload size, storage growth, and peak traffic. State assumptions explicitly.","Define APIs and core entities before drawing boxes. This makes access patterns and storage choices concrete.","Draw the simplest end-to-end architecture. Trace one read and one write, then add caches, queues, replicas, or partitions only when a requirement justifies them.","Close with failure modes and trade-offs: hot keys, duplicate requests, partial outages, overload, data loss, and operational complexity."]},
 {id:"url-shortener",title:"Design a URL shortener",track:"System design",type:"Problem breakdown",time:"30 min",level:"FOUNDATIONAL",desc:"Short-code generation, redirect latency, storage, abuse prevention, and analytics.",sections:["Clarify custom aliases, link expiry, editability, and analytics. Redirect traffic is typically much higher than link creation.","Expose create-link and redirect APIs. Generate collision-safe IDs, validate destination URLs, and make creation idempotent under retries.","Compare random IDs, counters, and encoded unique IDs against collision risk, coordination cost, predictability, and complexity.","Cache popular mappings and define invalidation for expired or deleted links. Protect the origin from hot keys.","Record click analytics asynchronously so telemetry does not add redirect latency. Discuss abuse detection, rate limits, and regional latency."]},
 {id:"rate-limiter",title:"Design a distributed rate limiter",track:"System design",type:"Problem breakdown",time:"25 min",level:"CORE PATTERN",desc:"Token bucket vs. sliding window, atomic counters, distributed enforcement, and fail-open choices.",sections:["Define the limiting key and policy: user, API key, IP, endpoint, quota, burst allowance, and global vs. regional scope.","Compare fixed window, sliding window, leaky bucket, and token bucket by burst behavior, precision, memory, and complexity.","Choose shared state or partitioned ownership for multiple instances. Discuss atomicity, expiry, races, and slow state stores.","Enforce at the edge when possible. Distinguish local protection from globally accurate quotas and account for cross-region coordination cost.","Choose degraded behavior explicitly: fail open for availability or fail closed to protect dependencies. Instrument allowed, rejected, errors, and policy drift."]},
 {id:"news-feed",title:"Design a social news feed",track:"System design",type:"Problem breakdown",time:"35 min",level:"INTERMEDIATE",desc:"Fan-out on write vs. read, ranking, pagination, celebrity hot spots, and cache strategy.",sections:["Clarify chronological vs. ranked feed, freshness, interactions, and traffic skew from popular accounts.","Model users, follows, posts, and feed entries around fast paginated reads; hydrate post and author details separately if needed.","Fan-out on write makes reads fast but creates write amplification. Fan-out on read avoids huge writes but increases read cost. A hybrid handles high-follower accounts.","Use cursor pagination to avoid unstable offsets. Define how deletes, privacy changes, and ranking updates propagate.","Move generation and ranking to async workers. Address duplicate delivery, backpressure, rebuilds, stale results, and degraded ranking."]},
 {id:"chat",title:"Design a real-time chat system",track:"System design",type:"Problem breakdown",time:"35 min",level:"INTERMEDIATE",desc:"Persistent connections, message ordering, delivery states, fan-out, and offline users.",sections:["Clarify one-to-one and group chat, history, delivery guarantees, presence, attachments, and group size.","Use WebSockets or another persistent channel for live delivery, with HTTP for history and connection setup. Gateways manage active connections.","Persist messages before acknowledging them. Define per-conversation ordering and how clients reconcile retries or reconnects.","Route messages to active recipients and queue notifications for offline users. Keep fan-out and push delivery separate from durable message storage.","Plan for reconnect storms, duplicate delivery, abuse controls, encryption requirements, and regional failures."]},
 {id:"storage",title:"Database selection for system design",track:"System design",type:"Core concept",time:"18 min",level:"CORE CONCEPT",desc:"Choose relational, key-value, document, wide-column, and search systems based on access patterns.",sections:["Start from access patterns, consistency, transaction boundaries, data shape, and operational constraints—not from popularity.","Relational databases fit structured entities and transactions. Key-value stores fit predictable key lookups. Document stores fit flexible aggregate-shaped records.","Wide-column stores can support high write throughput and partitioned access patterns; search engines serve text retrieval and relevance, not primary transactional truth.","Design indexes from query patterns and understand write amplification, cardinality, hot partitions, and storage growth.","Explain the trade-off and migration path. A credible answer states why a simpler option is insufficient."]},
 {id:"caching",title:"Caching strategies and invalidation",track:"System design",type:"Core concept",time:"16 min",level:"CORE CONCEPT",desc:"Cache-aside, TTLs, stampedes, hot keys, and consistency trade-offs.",sections:["Identify the repeated expensive read and acceptable staleness before introducing a cache.","Cache-aside is simple and flexible; write-through improves cache freshness at the cost of write-path coupling; write-behind changes durability and ordering semantics.","Use TTL jitter, request coalescing, stale-while-revalidate, and bounded retries to control stampedes.","Hot keys may need replication, local caches, or request spreading. Each option changes invalidation and consistency.","Measure hit rate, tail latency, evictions, stale reads, and origin load. A high hit rate is not useful if freshness is wrong."]},
 {id:"queues",title:"Queues, streams, retries, and idempotency",track:"System design",type:"Core concept",time:"19 min",level:"CORE CONCEPT",desc:"Decouple services without losing control of ordering, retries, and backpressure.",sections:["Use asynchronous messaging when work can be decoupled from the request or when buffering protects a downstream dependency.","A queue distributes tasks among workers; a log-based stream retains ordered events for independent consumers and replay.","At-least-once delivery means duplicates are possible. Make handlers idempotent with stable event IDs, deduplication, or conditional writes.","Use bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter, dead-letter handling, and clear poison-message policy.","Monitor lag, queue depth, processing time, retry rate, and oldest-message age. Backpressure is a product behavior, not just an infrastructure detail."]},
 {id:"coding-patterns",title:"Recognize the right coding pattern",track:"Coding interviews",type:"Guide",time:"22 min",level:"FOUNDATIONAL",desc:"Use constraints and input structure to choose between hashing, two pointers, sliding windows, heaps, and graph traversal.",sections:["Start with input size and constraints. An O(n²) approach may be fine for tiny inputs but impossible at hundreds of thousands of items.","Identify the structure of the problem: contiguous range suggests a window, sorted pair search suggests two pointers, repeated lookup suggests hashing, and dependencies suggest a graph.","State the invariant your algorithm maintains. For a sliding window, explain exactly when the left boundary moves; for a heap, explain what its top element represents.","Walk a small example, including empty inputs, duplicates, boundary values, and the largest constraints.","Analyze time and space complexity, then explain why the chosen pattern fits better than the nearest alternative."]},
 {id:"object-design",title:"Design extensible object-oriented APIs",track:"Low-level design",type:"Guide",time:"24 min",level:"FOUNDATIONAL",desc:"Model responsibilities, interfaces, state transitions, and extension points without overengineering.",sections:["Clarify use cases and changing requirements before choosing classes. Model domain concepts, not every noun in the prompt.","Assign each object a clear responsibility and define ownership of mutable state. Keep orchestration separate from domain behavior where it improves clarity.","Prefer composition and small interfaces when multiple variants or policies are expected. Avoid inheritance trees created only to reuse a few lines.","Make state transitions explicit and enforce invariants at the boundary where state changes.","Test normal flows, invalid transitions, and extension scenarios. Explain where the design is intentionally simple and what would justify adding another abstraction."]},
 {id:"ml-serving",title:"Design an online ML inference platform",track:"ML system design",type:"Problem breakdown",time:"30 min",level:"INTERMEDIATE",desc:"Connect feature freshness, model serving, latency budgets, rollout safety, and monitoring.",sections:["Clarify prediction quality, latency, throughput, freshness, availability, and cost. Separate online inference from offline training.","Trace data from event collection to feature generation, training data, model registry, and serving. Prevent training-serving skew by defining feature semantics consistently.","Choose synchronous or asynchronous inference based on the caller's latency budget. Consider batching, model replicas, accelerators, and fallback behavior.","Roll out models gradually with versioning, shadow traffic, canaries, and a fast rollback path. Keep model and feature versions observable.","Monitor service metrics and model quality: latency percentiles, errors, drift, feature freshness, calibration, and delayed labels. Define who acts when metrics regress."]},
 {id:"behavioral-stories",title:"Structure a strong behavioral interview story",track:"Behavioral",type:"Guide",time:"12 min",level:"FOUNDATIONAL",desc:"Explain context, personal actions, trade-offs, measurable impact, and lessons learned with precision.",sections:["Choose a real situation with a clear challenge and meaningful stakes. Make your own responsibility explicit rather than hiding behind team-level language.","Give only the context needed to understand the decision. Explain the constraints, competing priorities, and why the problem was difficult.","Spend most of the answer on your actions: options considered, people aligned, risks managed, and how you measured progress.","Quantify outcomes when possible, but distinguish measured results from estimates. Be candid about setbacks and what changed after feedback.","Close with the lesson and what you would do differently. Prepare for follow-ups on alternatives, conflict, ownership, and evidence of impact."]},
 {id:"ai-coding-workflow",title:"Work effectively in an AI-enabled coding round",track:"AI-enabled coding",type:"Practice guide",time:"25 min",level:"PRACTICAL",desc:"Inspect an unfamiliar codebase, scope a change, use an AI assistant deliberately, and validate the result.",sections:["Read the task and repository structure first. Locate entry points, tests, conventions, and related code before asking an assistant to make broad changes.","Turn the request into acceptance criteria and a small implementation plan. Ask focused questions grounded in the files you have inspected.","Review generated changes as a maintainer: correctness, security, edge cases, API compatibility, and unnecessary scope. Do not treat plausible output as verified.","Run targeted tests, then the broader relevant suite. Add tests for regressions and inspect failures rather than repeatedly prompting without understanding.","Summarize the implementation, trade-offs, known limitations, and verification evidence. Your judgment and validation matter as much as typing speed."]}
];
